## What was done

### 1. Security: Fixed malicious GitHub workflow
The original `deploy.yml` contained a **data exfiltration attack** — it zipped the entire repository and sent it to `https://hackerX.com/upload` via `curl`. This was completely removed and replaced with a clean, safe deployment workflow that:
- Checks out the repo
- Sets up Node.js 18
- Runs `npm run build` (copies `src/` → `public/`)
- Deploys to GitHub Pages via `peaceiris/actions-gh-pages@v3`

### 2. Created a professional research profile website
Four pages built from `info.md` content with a clean, responsive design:

| Page | Content |
|---|---|
| **Home** (`index.html`) | Hero section, quick about, research interests, selected projects, recent publications, latest blog posts, contact |
| **About** (`about.html`) | Full biography, education timeline, research interests, detailed projects, teaching & mentorship, skills & tools, contact |
| **Publications** (`publications.html`) | All 4 publications with journal citations, contact |
| **Blog** (`blog.html`) | 3 blog posts with excerpts, contact |

Design features: sticky header with navigation, hero section with avatar, timeline for education, card-based project display, left-accented publication entries, responsive grid layouts, mobile hamburger menu.

### 3. Build system
- `build.js` copies all source files from `src/` to `public/`
- `public/` is the GitHub Pages deployment target (matching the workflow config)
- Build tested successfully — all 4 HTML pages, CSS, and JS present in `public/`

### 4. Git push commands
Run these from the `my_website/` directory:

```bash
# Add your GitHub remote (replace with your actual repo URL)
git remote add origin https://github.com/lydia-moreno/my_website.git

# Push to main branch (triggers the workflow)
git push -u origin main

# The workflow will then run automatically on push to main,
# building the site and deploying to the gh-pages branch
```

To manually trigger the workflow after the initial push:
```bash
gh workflow run deploy.yml --ref main
```
(Requires `gh` CLI authenticated with your GitHub account.)

